## 1. The mathematical problem we want to solve

Let the WSST matrix be

\\[ T\_{j,n}=T(f_j,t_n), \\]

with frequencies \\(f_j\\) in Hz and times \\(t_n\\) in seconds.

A ridge is a sequence of frequency-bin indices,

\\[ r=(r_1,r_2,\ldots,r\_{N_t}), \\]

whose physical frequencies are

\\[ f_r(t_n)=f\_{r_n}. \\]

We want a trajectory that passes through strong time-frequency coefficients while varying smoothly enough to correspond to a physical oscillation.

### Recommended objective

Use

\\[ \boxed{ r^\*=\underset{r}{\operatorname{argmin}} \left[ \sum\_{n=1}^{N_t}D(r_n,n) + \sum\_{n=2}^{N_t} \lambda_n \left( \frac{q(f\_{r_n})-q(f\_{r\_{n-1}})}{\sigma_n} \right)^2 \right]. } \\]

Here:

- \\(D(j,n)\\) is the local observation cost.
- \\(q(f)=f\\) gives physical-frequency continuity.
- \\(q(f)=\log f\\) gives logarithmic-frequency continuity.
- \\(\sigma_n\\) provides a frequency-change scale.
- \\(\lambda_n\\) controls smoothness.

For uniform sampling, it is simplest to choose constant \\(\lambda\\) and \\(\sigma\\). For nonuniform sampling, the transition cost should account explicitly for \\(\Delta t_n=t_n-t\_{n-1}\\).

The important thing is that the penalty acts on physical frequency coordinates, not merely the difference between bin indices.

For example, penalizing \\((r_n-r\_{n-1})^2\\) would make the same 2 Hz change four times more expensive when the output grid spacing is halved. We should avoid that unintended dependence.

A suitable observation cost is

\\[ D(j,n)= -\log\left( \frac{|T\_{j,n}|^2}{E\_{\mathrm{ref},n}}+\epsilon \right), \\]

with documented zero handling and a scale-aware reference. This is similar to the negative-log-energy formulation used by Python's ridge extraction.&#x20;

