I would also add a hard jump-rate constraint:

\\[ \left| \frac{f\_{r_n}-f\_{r\_{n-1}}}{\Delta t_n} \right| \le J\_{\max}, \\]

where \\(J\_{\max}\\) is expressed in Hz/s.

This is especially useful for synthetic tests and BNS applications where reasonable chirp rates can be estimated.

One subtlety: an energy-maximizing path will still follow noise if the actual mode disappears. To avoid presenting those regions as physically reliable detections, we need a separate activity or validity criterion.

## 2. The DP recurrence

For each frequency bin \\(j\\) at time \\(n\\), define \\(C_n(j)\\) as the minimum accumulated cost of any valid path ending at that bin.

Initialize

\\[ C_1(j)=D(j,1). \\]

Then compute

\\[ \boxed{ C_n(j)=D(j,n)+ \min\_{k\in\mathcal A_n(j)} \left[ C\_{n-1}(k)+P_n(k,j) \right]. } \\]

Here \\(\mathcal A_n(j)\\) is the set of admissible predecessor bins, and

\\[ P_n(k,j)= \lambda \left( \frac{q(f_j)-q(f_k)}{\sigma} \right)^2 \\]

is the continuity penalty.

Store the minimizing predecessor for every state.

After processing the final time sample, choose the minimum-cost final state and backtrack to obtain the optimal path.

This is the standard Viterbi-style dynamic program for a first-order trajectory model. With a fixed cost function and admissible transitions, it gives the global optimum of that discrete problem.

I strongly recommend making this recurrence the canonical specification. The optimized algorithms must reproduce its answer exactly, not merely produce a visually similar ridge.

