## Phase 9 — Ridge activity, gaps, and boundaries

A global DP normally returns a state at every time sample, even when no physical component is present.

Implement an explicit distinction between:

- A mathematically optimized path.
- A sufficiently supported physical ridge observation.
- A low-confidence or inactive interval.
- An excluded boundary interval.

Start with post-tracking activity masks derived from local SST evidence.

Possible evidence includes:

- Absolute coefficient magnitude.
- Relative coefficient magnitude.
- Local peak prominence.
- Energy contrast against a defined background.
- Consistency of the candidate ridge with surrounding frequency-band structure.

Do not interpret a heuristic score as a calibrated statistical probability.

Provide a clear policy for missing intervals:

- Mark the ridge inactive.
- Optionally interpolate frequency for visualization only.
- Do not label interpolated values as measured.
- Exclude inactive samples from frequency-error statistics.
- Do not silently synthesize an oscillatory component from a ridge-only extrapolation.

Explicit birth/death-state modeling or globally optimal gap transitions are optional, not mandatory for this milestone. If added, define their objective and validate it with exhaustive cases.

Expose boundary/reliability masks from the underlying WSST.

The tracker must not silently treat boundary artifacts as equally trustworthy as valid interior coefficients.

