## Phase 12 — Overlap ownership and residual consistency

Two ridge bands may select the same SST bin.

Simply summing both bands would double-count its complex coefficient.

Implement at least two ownership policies:

1. Exclusive nearest-ridge assignment within eligible bands.
2. Fractional partition of overlapping bins with nonnegative weights.

For each frequency-time bin, define ownership weights α_k(j,n) for components k and a residual weight α_res(j,n).

Require:

```
α_k(j,n) >= 0
α_res(j,n) >= 0

Σ_k α_k(j,n) + α_res(j,n) = 1
```

Then define:

```
T_k(j,n) = α_k(j,n) T(j,n)

T_res(j,n) = α_res(j,n) T(j,n)
```

This guarantees complex coefficient conservation:

```
Σ_k T_k + T_res = T
```

and ensures no double counting.

The simplest default should be exclusive nearest-ridge ownership, with deterministic tie handling.

Fractional ownership may help represent overlap smoothly but must not be presented as physically unique mode separation.

Make component masks and ownership weights inspectable in the result.

Verify that component and residual synthesis sums reproduce the full standard SST synthesis within floating-point tolerance.

Distinguish the SST-domain residual from any low-frequency or rejected-coefficient residual maintained elsewhere.

