## Phase 16 — Numerical definitions and evaluation conventions

Define frequency RMSE on the set of valid samples Ω:

```
RMSE_f = sqrt(mean((f_est[n]-f_true[n])² for n in Ω))
```

Report coverage separately:

```
coverage = |Ω| / N_expected
```

Do not reduce RMSE merely by marking difficult samples invalid.

Define relative reconstruction error:

```
Erel = norm(xrec-xtrue) / norm(xtrue)
```

Define:

```
SRER = 10 log10(
    sum(abs2, xtrue) /
    sum(abs2, xrec-xtrue)
)
```

Use dB for SRER.

For phase error, use an explicitly documented phase convention and compare only where the reference amplitude is sufficiently large for phase to be meaningful.

For real signals, use a consistent analytic-signal or phase-estimation convention.

For complex signals, distinguish phase error from a constant global phase offset; report both raw and globally phase-aligned metrics if alignment is allowed.

Do not allow hidden amplitude or phase fitting in the primary reconstruction-error metric.

Define residual metrics separately for:

- Unassigned SST complex coefficients.
- Reconstructed unassigned oscillatory waveform.
- Actual difference between full-input signal and summed reconstructions.
- DC and other contributions outside oscillatory WSST reconstruction.

Do not refer to all of these as the same residual.

Use coefficient mass or norm descriptors precisely. Squared magnitudes of SST bins are not automatically conserved physical signal energy.

