## 4. Important design decisions

There are three decisions I would settle now rather than leaving them to the agent.

### Decision A — Use global DP as the scientific default

There is a subtle distinction between two implementations.

`ssqueezepy` implements a forward-backward ridge tracking algorithm based on negative-log normalized energy and a quadratic continuity penalty. Its source is a useful reference, but our goal should be an explicitly defined global discrete-path optimum, independently verified by brute force.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://github.com\&sz=32)

GitHub



The recommended hierarchy is:

| Algorithm                    | Role                                                       | Complexity                  |
| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Maximum-energy per column    | Baseline                                                   | \\(O(N_tN_f)\\)             |
| Dense DP                     | Correctness reference                                      | \\(O(N_tN_f^2)\\)           |
| Banded DP                    | Default production method when transitions are constrained | \\(O(N_tN_fB)\\)            |
| Quadratic distance transform | Optional optimization when assumptions hold                | Potentially \\(O(N_tN_f)\\) |

Here \\(B\\) is the maximum number of allowed predecessor bins per state.

Use banded DP as the initial production implementation, with a configurable physical jump-rate limit. It is relatively simple, efficient, and sufficient for the anticipated signal sizes.

The quadratic-distance-transform optimization should remain optional. We should not spend disproportionate development effort on it before establishing reconstruction quality.

### Decision B — Use physical frequency as the default coordinate

Your package supports linear, logarithmic, and arbitrary frequency grids.

The tracker should not depend on the grid parameterization unless explicitly requested.

For the BNS analysis, I recommend initially using

\\[ q(f)=f,\qquad P(k,j)=\lambda \left(\frac{f_j-f_k}{\sigma_f}\right)^2, \\]

with a separately configured physical maximum frequency-change rate.

This makes parameter interpretation straightforward.

Log-frequency penalties remain useful when tracking oscillations spanning several octaves, but I would not make them the default for post-merger modes occupying a relatively restricted physical band.

### Decision C — Do not automatically fit reconstruction bands from the ridge

A ridge gives an estimate of instantaneous frequency:

\\[ f_k(t). \\]

It does not uniquely determine how much of the time-frequency plane belongs to that physical component.

The reconstruction bandwidth must be treated as a distinct parameter.

For initial tests, I recommend

\\[ B_k(t)= [f_k(t)-\Delta f_k,\ f_k(t)+\Delta f_k]. \\]

Then sweep \\(\Delta f_k\\) while measuring reconstruction errors against known synthetic components.

This allows us to quantify whether a poorly reconstructed mode is caused by inaccurate tracking or an inadequate reconstruction band.

For actual numerical-relativity waveforms, where the component ground truth is not known, reconstruction-band sensitivity becomes an important systematic uncertainty.

## 5. Specific numerical experiments I would prioritize

Experiment 1 — Why DP is better than greedy

Construct two ridges whose amplitudes alternate, while their frequencies remain well separated. The column-wise maximum will jump between them; the DP should remain on one trajectory when continuity constraints justify it.

Test this entirely on a synthetic time-frequency matrix before involving the CWT.

Experiment 2 — What happens at a crossing?

Construct two chirps crossing at a known time. Test whether sequential extraction exchanges their identities.

A continuous output trajectory is not sufficient evidence of correct tracking. Record the fraction of time assigned to the correct physical component and the number of identity swaps.

Experiment 3 — Ridge accuracy versus waveform reconstruction

First reconstruct a component using its known analytical frequency trajectory. Then reconstruct it using the extracted ridge.

The difference in reconstruction error isolates the effect of ridge estimation, holding the band-selection rule fixed.

Experiment 4 — Grid invariance

Use 160, 320, and 640 output frequency bins, while maintaining the same signal and physical penalties.

Frequency RMSE and reconstructed components should converge, rather than changing drastically because the bin width changed.

## 6. Should we implement more sophisticated multiridge tracking now?

Not yet.

The sequential approach in your original milestone is sensible as a first implementation. More elaborate joint multi-ridge optimization could address some identity-switching and exclusion failures, but it also introduces a substantially harder optimization problem.

The ridge-extraction literature identifies mode selection and separation as nontrivial problems even when time-frequency representations are well concentrated.&#x20;

